[
 [
  "h2",
  "Executive summary"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "NOTE: CURRENTLY IN DRAFT. WILL COMPLETE THIS SECTION ONCE SECTIONS 7&amp;8 ARE COMPLETE."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "We will spend around €4.8m acquiring face-to-face agency donors in Germany this year. Those donors should give us €11.9m over the next ten years, <b>€7.0m in net income</b>."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Many things shape a donor’s LTV, but two matter most: <b>what we ask donors for</b>, and <b>who we ask</b>."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "What we ask for"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "<b>€20 is the best acquisition value for three of our four agencies, on gross and net LTV alike</b>. It costs more to acquire, but the larger gift and strong retention more than cover it: <b>€617 net LTV over 10 years against €368 at €10.</b> That holds within every age band we can measure, so it is not an effect of €20 happening to attract older donors. However, the picture is more nuanced when we look at other acquisition values, we sometimes see differing pictures across age bands. For the under 30s for example, an annual gift of €60 (shown in the report as €5 per month) nets €128, whilst a gift of €12–13 a month, nets us €100 over 10 years."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "Who we ask"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "<b>Age is the strongest single predictor of value</b>. On the same €10 ask, a donor acquired at 65 or over is worth €793 over ten years against €341 for one acquired under 30. It is why the same €20 ask is worth €1,112 at Trust and €882 at Apollon: only 4% of Trust’s donors are under 30, against 40% of Apollon’s."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "<b>Interestingly, half of every under-30 we acquire comes from Apollon, and a quarter from Wesser</b> - our two most expensive rates buying our least valuable age group."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "The €20 fee cap"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "<b>The cap at Trust and Activate saves us about €15,000 a year</b>. Less than 1% of their donors are acquired above €20, and with no fee to gain the agencies have little reason to ask for more. We cannot prove from the data that the cap is the cause, but it’s worth noting <b>Apollon, an agency without the fee cap, have proportionally acquired 4x the donors giving above €20 compared to Activate, and 10x compared to Trust.</b>"
 ],
 [
  "h2",
  "Methodology"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "We take the donors our agencies acquired in Q1–Q2 2026, assume the same level of acquisitions in Q3–Q4 2026 and follow that group forward ten years, using how donors behaved 2022–2024 to allow us to factor in retention rates and upgrade rates. Costs come from the 2026 fee schedule, applied donor by donor at the acquiring agency’s own rate."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "At the moment, acquisition strategy and gift values vary across the four agencies in Germany. Whilst we do look at an overall net LTV by using a blended CPA, it is also important to consider each agency separately, since each agency has different cost structures. The scenario modelling in section 7 will therefore look at how we can optimise income on an agency by agency basis."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "The 2026 cohort is considerably older than 2022–2024, whom we model behaviour from — 44 years on average against 39, with 18% under 30 rather than 36%. The agencies also differ: Trust and Activate acquire donors averaging 47, Apollon and Wesser 38 and 39. Using historic behaviour unadjusted would understate this year’s donors, and comparing agencies without adjusting would credit Trust for being cheap when part of it is that they acquire older donors."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "In the model, if Apollon moved donors to €20, we assume those donors stay Apollon’s age, they do not turn into Trust’s average donor age. Ultimately, we are assuming agencies continue to acquire at the current age levels."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "We were not able to link back all donors to an agency, we had particularly low coverage for X agency donors [SN TO ADD AGENCY NAME]. We therefore built each agency’s gift mix and age profile from the donors we could attribute, scaling those patterns up to the full-year volumes."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Historic data was limited for non-monthly donors. Assumptions have been made about the behaviour of these donors and can be found in the appendix."
 ],
 [
  "note",
  "On the 10 year horizon - years 1 to 5 are observed based on historic donor trends. Years 6 to 10 are projected."
 ],
 [
  "h2",
